// Wonder Lab ancient history data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py
// Years are never `found` — see check_history and .work/BRIEF-ancient.md.
// 42 facts across 7 sections.
const ANCIENT = [{"id":"greece-1","section":"greece","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The Parthenon has almost no straight lines in it. The floor rises about 4 inches in the middle of the long sides. The columns lean slightly inwards and bulge in the middle. Measure it and you find hardly anything is truly flat or upright.","more":"Why is argued about — a correction for how eyes work, or simply how it looked best. The measurements themselves are not in doubt; people have surveyed that building for two hundred years."},{"id":"greece-2","section":"greece","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"Sponge divers working a shipwreck off Antikythera in 1901 brought up a corroded green lump. Inside it were bronze gears — at least thirty of them, cut with triangular teeth, meshed together.","more":"X-rays and scans have since read the inscriptions inside it. It tracked the sun and moon and predicted eclipses. Nothing else of that complexity survives from anywhere near its time."},{"id":"greece-3","section":"greece","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"Archimedes worked out how levers multiply force and is quoted as saying that with a place to stand he could move the earth. He also designed a screw inside a tube that lifts water uphill when you turn it. Farmers still use that design.","tryit":"Balance a ruler on a pencil with a book on one end. Move the pencil closer to the book and press the other end. Notice how much less effort it takes the further away you push."},{"id":"greece-4","section":"greece","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"The Greeks borrowed their letters from the Phoenicians and added something the Phoenicians did not write: vowels. Our A, B and E are those same letters, passed on through the Romans, with the shapes worn smooth by a few thousand years of copying.","more":"Alpha and beta are Greek letter names. Put them together and you have the word alphabet."},{"id":"greece-5","section":"greece","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The Greeks kept written lists of who won at the Olympic Games, event by event. The prize was a wreath of wild olive. There was no second place recorded.","more":"Those winner-lists are useful far beyond sport. Because the games were held on a fixed cycle, Greek writers dated other events by counting games — which gives historians a ruler to measure other records against."},{"id":"greece-6","section":"greece","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"The story that a runner carried news from Marathon to Athens and died is not in the earliest account. Herodotus, writing closest to the event, describes a runner going the other way — to Sparta, to ask for help, a much longer run.","more":"The dying-messenger version turns up centuries later. It might be true. But when the earliest writer tells a different story, that is worth knowing before you repeat the famous one."},{"id":"rome-1","section":"rome","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"Roman concrete harbors are still standing in seawater, which modern concrete does not manage. The Romans mixed volcanic ash into theirs. Seawater reacts with that ash and grows new crystals inside the concrete, so it gets stronger instead of crumbling.","more":"This was worked out by drilling cores out of Roman harbors and looking at them under microscopes. The recipe was never lost, exactly — it was written down by Vitruvius. It just stopped being used."},{"id":"rome-2","section":"rome","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The Pantheon in Rome is roofed by a concrete dome 142 ft across with a 27 ft hole open to the sky in the middle. There is no steel in it. It is still the largest unreinforced concrete dome in the world, and the rain still comes in.","more":"The concrete gets lighter toward the top — heavy stone in the mix at the bottom, light volcanic rock near the opening. There are drains in the floor."},{"id":"rome-3","section":"rome","cat":"howworks","kind":"found","text":"The aqueduct that fed the Roman town of Nîmes runs about 31 miles and drops only 41 ft over that whole distance. That is a slope of roughly one inch in every 250 ft. They surveyed it with water levels and sighting rods.","more":"Too steep and the water tears the channel apart; too flat and it stops. The Pont du Gard, the bridge that carries it across a valley, is 160 ft tall and still standing."},{"id":"rome-4","section":"rome","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"When Vesuvius buried Pompeii, bodies left holes in the hardened ash as they decayed. In 1863 an excavator started pouring plaster into the holes before digging them out. What comes out is the shape of a person in their last moment.","more":"Pliny the Younger watched the eruption from across the bay as a teenager and wrote two letters describing it. His account and the ash layers match."},{"id":"rome-5","section":"rome","cat":"daily","kind":"found","text":"The Colosseum held around 50,000 people and had 80 entrances. Spectators were given numbered tokens telling them which arch to use and where to sit. The numbers are still carved above the arches.","more":"It could be emptied fast, which is the hard part of any big building. Modern stadiums are laid out on the same principle."},{"id":"rome-6","section":"rome","cat":"writing","kind":"worked","text":"A surprising share of ordinary English words came from Latin by way of French. Animal, family, question, city, table, letter, number. The short blunt words are usually the older English ones: house, food, eat, work.","tryit":"Read a page of any book and try to sort the words into short blunt ones and long smooth ones. The long ones are mostly Latin arriving late."},{"id":"egypt-1","section":"egypt","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The Great Pyramid's base covers 13 acres and its four sides differ in length by less than 8 inches. The ground it sits on was levelled to within about an inch across the whole thing. It was built 455 ft high and has lost about 26 ft off the top.","more":"Those are measurements anyone can go and check, and people have, repeatedly, with better instruments each time. The precision is the astonishing part, not the size."},{"id":"egypt-2","section":"egypt","cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"You will often read that the Great Pyramid contains 2.3 million blocks. Nobody has counted them. The figure comes from measuring the outside, working out the volume, and dividing by the average size of the blocks you can see.","more":"It is a reasonable estimate and it might be well out — nobody knows how much of the inside is solid rock left in place rather than cut blocks."},{"id":"egypt-3","section":"egypt","cat":"when","kind":"worked","text":"Most books date the Great Pyramid to about 2560 BC. That number is built from Egyptian king-lists — copies made centuries later of records of who reigned and for how long — lined up with carbon dates from mortar. Nothing on the pyramid gives a year.","more":"On the Bible's own timeline, adding up the genealogies, that would fall within a few centuries after the Flood, and Ussher's reckoning would not allow it that early at all. Both numbers are worked out. Neither was read off the stone.","tryit":"Ask someone how they know when something old was made. If the answer is just \"they worked it out\", ask what they worked it out from. That is always the interesting part."},{"id":"egypt-4","section":"egypt","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In room 4 of the British Museum there is a slab of dark stone with the same decree cut into it three times, in three different scripts. French soldiers dug it out of a wall at Rashid in 1799. It is the Rosetta Stone.","more":"Because one of the three was Greek, which people could still read, it became the key to hieroglyphs. Jean-François Champollion announced he had cracked them in 1822."},{"id":"egypt-5","section":"egypt","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"Hieroglyphs are not picture-writing in the way people expect. Most of the signs stand for sounds, not things. A king's name is written inside an oval loop called a cartouche, which is how Champollion got his first foothold — he went looking for Ptolemy.","tryit":"Look up a hieroglyph alphabet chart and write your own name in a cartouche. The signs for sounds English does not have will make you improvise, which is exactly what the Egyptians did with foreign names."},{"id":"egypt-6","section":"egypt","cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"To make a mummy, the body was packed in natron — a natural salt — for about forty days to dry it out. The lungs, liver, stomach and intestines were taken out and stored separately. The brain was removed through the nose with a hooked rod and thrown away.","more":"The heart was left in. They thought that was where a person did their thinking, so it had to stay. We know the method from Egyptian texts and from unwrapping mummies and looking."},{"id":"mesopot-1","section":"mesopot","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"The oldest writing we can still read was pressed into wet clay with the cut end of a reed. Each press leaves a wedge, which is why it is called cuneiform — Latin for wedge-shaped. Hundreds of thousands of these tablets have been dug up.","more":"Most have never been translated. There are more untranslated cuneiform tablets sitting in museum drawers than there are translated ones.","tryit":"Flatten a lump of plasticine and press a popsicle stick end into it at different angles. Try to make a mark you could tell apart from every other mark you have made. It is harder than it looks."},{"id":"mesopot-2","section":"mesopot","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In the Louvre stands a finger of black stone 7 ft 5 in tall with 282 laws cut around it — the Code of Hammurabi. It was found not in Babylon but at Susa in Iran, in 1901, because somebody had carried it off as loot.","more":"The laws cover farm rent, wages, surgery gone wrong, and what happens if a house falls down and kills someone. It is one of the oldest legal documents anybody can read."},{"id":"mesopot-3","section":"mesopot","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"The ziggurat at Ur in southern Iraq is a stepped mud-brick platform with three great staircases meeting at the top. The lowest stage is still standing and has been rebuilt. People climb those stairs today.","more":"It is made of millions of mud bricks with a baked-brick skin held together with tar. Some of the bricks are stamped with a king's name, which is a bit like finding a builder's mark."},{"id":"mesopot-4","section":"mesopot","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The Standard of Ur is a wooden box about 19 in long, covered in shell and blue lapis lazuli, showing rows of little figures. One side shows a feast. The other shows wagons — four solid wheels each, pulled by donkeys.","more":"It came out of a royal tomb dug by Leonard Woolley in the 1920s and is in the British Museum. Nobody actually knows what it was for."},{"id":"mesopot-5","section":"mesopot","cat":"copied","kind":"found","text":"The earliest wheels that survive are not on carts. They are potter's wheels — flat discs spun to shape clay. Wheels for carrying things show up later, made of three planks pegged together into a solid disc, not spokes.","more":"Spoked wheels came much later and changed everything, because a solid wooden wheel is enormously heavy and a spoked one is not."},{"id":"mesopot-6","section":"mesopot","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"At Nineveh, archaeologists found the remains of a king's library — around 30,000 clay tablets and fragments collected by Ashurbanipal. Letters, omens, medicine, word-lists, and the poem carrying the flood account.","more":"The palace burned down. Fire destroys paper and parchment; it bakes clay harder. The library survived because it was destroyed."},{"id":"beginning-1","section":"beginning","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Genesis opens with six days of making and a seventh of rest. Light, sky, sea and land, plants, sun and moon and stars, fish and birds, land animals and people. It is written as a sequence of days, each one closing with an evening and a morning.","more":"Genesis is a written record. You can go and read it, which is a different kind of thing from a measurement or a calculation."},{"id":"beginning-2","section":"beginning","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 5 is a list. Ten men from Adam to Noah, and for each one it gives the age he was when his son was born and how long he lived after. Genesis 11 does the same thing again from Noah's son Shem down to Abraham.","more":"Lists like this are why a timeline is possible at all. Most ancient writing does not tell you how many years passed between one man and the next."},{"id":"beginning-3","section":"beginning","cat":"when","kind":"worked","text":"Add those two lists up and you get a number of years from creation to Abraham. James Ussher, an Irish archbishop, did exactly that and published his answer in 1650: creation on the night before the 23rd of October, 4004 BC.","more":"That date is arithmetic, not a verse. Nowhere does the Bible say 4004. Ussher added the genealogies, then anchored the total to a known Babylonian king's death that could be tied to a dated eclipse.","tryit":"Add up your own family instead. How old was your mother when you were born? Her mother when she was born? Keep going back as far as anyone can remember and see how many years you get."},{"id":"beginning-4","section":"beginning","cat":"writing","kind":"worked","text":"Ussher's total is not the only one, because the old copies do not all carry the same numbers. The Greek Septuagint gives longer gaps in the same genealogies, and adding those up puts creation nearer 5500 BC — about 1,500 years earlier.","more":"Both are honest arithmetic on a written list. They differ because the lists differ. Anyone who gives you one number without mentioning that is leaving something out."},{"id":"beginning-5","section":"beginning","cat":"artifact","kind":"record","text":"The oldest complete Hebrew Bible that still exists is the Leningrad Codex, copied by hand in AD 1008. It is in the Russian National Library in St Petersburg. Older still is the Aleppo Codex, from around AD 930, but parts of it are missing.","more":"The scribe signed and dated the book at the back, which is how the year is known. Every printed Hebrew Bible today is set from this manuscript."},{"id":"beginning-6","section":"beginning","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The oldest known piece of the Bible is small enough to fit in your palm. Two rolled silver strips, found in a burial cave at Ketef Hinnom in Jerusalem in 1979, carry the priestly blessing from Numbers 6 scratched into the metal.","more":"They were so fragile it took three years to work out how to unroll them without destroying them. They are in the Israel Museum."},{"id":"flood-1","section":"flood","cat":"size","kind":"record","text":"Genesis gives the Ark's measurements: 300 cubits long, 50 wide, 30 high. A cubit is about 18 inches, so that is roughly 450 ft long, 75 ft wide and 45 ft high — longer than a football field and about as tall as a four-story house.","more":"Those are the numbers in the text. The shape is not described beyond a door, a roof and three decks, so every picture you have ever seen of it involves somebody guessing.","tryit":"Take a tape measure outside and mark out 450 ft. Walk from one end to the other and count your steps. That is one boat."},{"id":"flood-2","section":"flood","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In the British Museum there is a broken lump of clay about the size of a mobile phone, covered in wedge marks. It tells of a great flood, a boat, and birds sent out to find land. It was dug up at Nineveh and is called the Flood Tablet.","more":"It is part of the Epic of Gilgamesh, a Babylonian poem. The flood account on it is not the same as Genesis — different names, different gods, different boat — but it is unmistakably a story about the same kind of event."},{"id":"flood-3","section":"flood","cat":"people","kind":"record","text":"George Smith worked at the British Museum sorting tablets nobody had read. In 1872 he made out the flood lines on one of them and, according to a colleague who was in the room, became so excited that he jumped up and started taking his clothes off.","more":"He then traveled to Iraq to look for the missing piece of the tablet, and found it. That almost never happens."},{"id":"flood-4","section":"flood","cat":"record","kind":"worked","text":"Flood accounts turn up in the written traditions of peoples all over the world, in places that had no contact with each other. Babylon, Greece, India, China, and among peoples across the Americas and the Pacific.","more":"What that means is argued about. Some say it points back to one real event remembered everywhere; others say big floods happen everywhere and so do stories about them. The written accounts themselves are simply there, in libraries, and you can read them."},{"id":"flood-5","section":"flood","cat":"writing","kind":"record","text":"Genesis 11 says that at Babel the whole earth had one language, and that afterwards people could not understand one another and scattered. The tower is described as brick and tar, not stone.","more":"The detail about brick is the kind of thing that only matters if you know the region. Southern Iraq has almost no building stone. Everything there really was built of mud brick."},{"id":"flood-6","section":"flood","cat":"howworks","kind":"worked","text":"Languages sort into families. English, Greek, Hindi and Farsi share so many words and word-endings that they are grouped together as one family. So are Hebrew and Arabic, in a different one. Nobody has managed to link all the families to a single root.","more":"Families are worked out by comparing words across hundreds of languages and looking for patterns that hold every time. Latin pater and English father. Latin pes and English foot. Whether the families join up further back is not something anyone has shown."},{"id":"israel-1","section":"israel","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In 1993 a broken piece of black basalt turned up in a wall at Tel Dan in northern Israel. Cut into it, in Aramaic, is a boast by a king about defeating his enemies — and the phrase \"House of David\".","more":"It is the earliest mention of David found outside the Bible. Before it was dug up, some argued he was entirely legendary. The stone is in the Israel Museum in Jerusalem."},{"id":"israel-2","section":"israel","cat":"built","kind":"found","text":"Under Jerusalem there is a tunnel 1,750 ft long, cut through solid rock, that still carries water. It was dug to bring a spring inside the city walls before a siege. You can walk it today, in the water, with a flashlight.","more":"Two teams cut it, starting from opposite ends. The tunnel wanders in an S-shape, and the pick marks change direction where they met.","tryit":"Measure out 1,750 ft on a map of your own street. That is how far two teams cut through rock, in the dark, and still met."},{"id":"israel-3","section":"israel","cat":"writing","kind":"found","text":"In 1880 a boy wading in that tunnel noticed writing on the wall. It describes the last moments of the dig: the two teams calling to each other through the rock, then the pick-axes breaking through, and the water running.","more":"The inscription was cut out of the wall and is now in the Archaeology Museum in Istanbul. It is one of very few ancient inscriptions written by the workmen rather than about a king."},{"id":"israel-4","section":"israel","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"An Assyrian king had the siege of the town of Lachish carved along the walls of his palace at Nineveh — siege ramps, archers, battering rams, and the people leaving. The whole wall is now in the British Museum.","more":"Excavators later dug at Lachish itself and found the siege ramp, the burned layer, and heaps of sling stones and arrowheads. The carving and the ground agree with each other."},{"id":"israel-5","section":"israel","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"In 1947 a shepherd threw a stone into a cave near the Dead Sea and heard pottery break. Inside were jars of scrolls. One is a complete book of Isaiah, 24 ft long, written on 17 sheets of leather sewn together.","more":"It is displayed in a building in Jerusalem shaped like the lid of the jars. What it showed is that the text had been copied for a very long time with remarkably few changes."},{"id":"israel-6","section":"israel","cat":"artifact","kind":"found","text":"The Cyrus Cylinder is a barrel of clay about 9 in long, covered in cuneiform, dug up at Babylon in 1879. On it, King Cyrus says he sent captured peoples back to their own homes and let them rebuild their temples.","more":"That is the same policy the book of Ezra describes from the other side. The cylinder does not mention Judah by name. What it shows is that Cyrus really did work this way."}];
globalThis.ANCIENT = ANCIENT;
